name o2-05-late-radar-1
timeout 400
time 1984-06-14T06:00:00Z

# CAMPAIGN O2, SORTIE 5 OF 10 - PVO INTERCEPT EXERCISE: GROUND CONTROL IN ITS PURE FORM.
# THE COMMIT IS LATE: THE CONTROLLER HOLDS HIS FIGHTER SILENT UNTIL 20 KM.
# Spec, anchor and sources: doc/campaigns/o2-pvo-intercept.md. Format: doc/missions/campaign.md.
# CONTROL: o2-01-vector.fbm - identical but for the TIMES and the RANGES of the six `set brief_gci` lines.
#
# THE ARENA, and every coordinate in it is [SET]. An exercise has no battlefield, so this campaign flies
# over the Soviet air-defence firing range at Ashuluk, Astrakhan oblast [T4], taken as ~47.30 N 47.90 E
# and NOT improved on. Flown under `--elev const` on a 0 m datum and with NO TERRAIN MASKING (gap C4).
#   the defended complex   a command post (target_hard) and a vehicle park (target_soft) - the objects
#                          the vector exists to protect
#   the early-warning set  a P-18, `set emcon free`, deployed forward of the complex on the approach
#                          axis. GAP C1 IS CLOSED AND THIS CAMPAIGN RE-CHECKED IT: the spec still says
#                          "target_soft only, the EW radar that generates the vector is inert". It is
#                          not inert any more - it radiates, it is heard by the intruder's RWR as
#                          `kind=surface-search` from t = 0.1, it builds its own tracks, and it can be
#                          killed. Every ground object in this campaign is OURS and none is a target
# Scale at this latitude: 1 deg of longitude = 75 490 m, 1 deg of latitude = 111 132 m [DERIVED].
#
# TEMPORAL HONESTY AND ITS DIRECTION. The PVO's own subject was a large, slow, high, unmanoeuvring
# aircraft - a bomber or an ELINT platform - and FlightBox has neither as a flyable module (gap C7: the
# catalogue's `tu95` row is a KINEMATIC MOVER, not an airframe). The intruder here is an F-16: faster,
# smaller and far harder to find than the aircraft the doctrine was written against. THE SUBSTITUTION
# MAKES THE INTERCEPT HARDER THAN HISTORY, so a FlightBox result in which the loop still closes says
# something WEAKER than the doctrine claimed, and one in which it does not close says nothing about it.
#
# AND THE CATALOGUE IS NOT A WAY ROUND IT. Four `air` rows are ACCEPTED, and ACCEPTED in
# doc/modules/air/flight-model-recipe.md means ACCEPTED AS A FLIGHT MODEL - the promotion gate measures
# the DECK and nothing else. It measures no weapon, no sensor and no engagement, and four of the ten
# armed rows cannot employ anything at all. A campaign citing a catalogue row as a COMBATANT would be
# citing something no gate has ever checked, so this campaign cites none: every aircraft in all ten
# files is one of the two flyable modules.
#
# THE STRUCTURAL DISCLOSURE, repeated in all ten files because a reader who skips it reads every result
# backwards:
#   1. THE CONTROLLER IS A STRING (gap C6). `set brief_gci` is fixed before the run: nothing re-vectors,
#      nothing goes silent mid-intercept, nothing is wrong halfway through. Every wrong brief in this
#      campaign is wrong FROM THE START, which is the easy case, and the campaign says so.
#   2. THE PILOT CANNOT DISAGREE WITH HIS BRIEF, AND WHAT LOOKS LIKE A RECOVERY IS A DEAD-BAND ACCIDENT.
#      `FBMig29Pilot` types what it was given and has no path to re-enter a call it has reason to doubt.
#      `FBPilot`'s own uncued search law does keep posting an elevation, but only when ITS OWN wanted
#      angle has moved more than `kInterceptElDeadDeg` = 2.0 deg from ITS OWN last command - and that
#      cache is per aircraft. MEASURED in sortie 02: the wingman's cache sat at 0.0, drifted past the
#      band at t = 55.9 and pulled the antenna back down; the leader's sat at -2.0 from t = 3.5, never
#      drifted, and never looked at the raid again. One aircraft of two recovered, 28 s late, and not
#      because anybody doubted the controller. So 02 and 03 measure the cost of a wrong entry AND the
#      absence of any deliberate recovery, and the two cannot be separated inside one file.
#   3. THE GCI ELEVATION ENTRY IS A WORLD-FRAME ANGLE IN A BODY-FRAME COMMAND (doc/pilot.md 2.15, found
#      by O5 and STILL OPEN on the tree this campaign was flown against). Every interceptor in all ten
#      files is therefore laid out in LEVEL flight, and what that costs is measured in this campaign's
#      own attribution run rather than argued (see doc/campaigns/o2-pvo-intercept.md, State).
#   4. `FBMissionRunner` ends the whole run at the first flight-monitor K.O. (`FirstFlightKo`).
#
# THE ONE TACTICAL QUESTION: the whole point of a late emission is measured ON THE OTHER JET, and
# cross-unit measurement is this campaign's METHOD here rather than its problem. The declared range is
# 20 km: the controller says nothing until the raid is inside it, so the barrier's first entry falls at
# t = 65 s instead of t = 20 s. HOW MUCH SURPRISE DOES THAT BUY, IN SECONDS AND IN KILOMETRES - and
# what does it cost in the only currency an intercept has, the time left to shoot?
#   The intruder's warning receiver is the instrument. It already carries the P-18 as
#   `kind=surface-search` from t = 0.1, so the two emitters are separated in the log by their KIND and
#   not by a bearing argument: the barrier appears as `kind=fire-control`, 0.1 s after `n019 EMISSION`.
#
# THE ONE LEVER: the commit time. (20 s, 38 km) (50 s, 25 km) (80 s, 12 km) -> (65 s, 19 km)
# (80 s, 12 km) (90 s, 8 km). It moves three numbers per line and it is ONE lever, because a call made
# later necessarily reports the range that is true then; a late call carrying the old range would be a
# WRONG call, which is sortie 02's and 03's experiment and not this one's.
#
# READING RULE (binding): read `rwr THREAT_NEW unit=p5int kind=fire-control` - its timestamp is the
# answer - and convert it to a range from the two telemetry files' positions at that tick. Then read
# `radar RADAR_CONTACT`, `sms RELEASE` and any `stores DETONATION`, and compare all four against
# o2-01. exit 3 expected.
#
# MEASURED (fingerprint 9e637d7baa2a6c13, exit 3): THE LATE COMMIT BUYS 45.3 SECONDS AND COSTS THE
# WHOLE ENGAGEMENT.
#   gci BRAA t=64.9 -> n019 EMISSION t=73.2 (8.3 s, the chain's own cost, 0.3 s above the control's 8.0
#   because one entry waited a decision tick longer) -> first firm track t=76.3 at 3.68 nm (6.82 km).
#   THE PURCHASE, measured on the OTHER jet: `rwr THREAT_NEW unit=p5int kind=fire-control` at t=73.3
#   against t=28.0 in the control - 45.3 s in which the intruder heard only the ground.
#   THE PRICE, measured on this one: the first track comes at 6.82 km instead of 29.78 km, i.e. 23.0 km
#   and 77 % of the detection range are gone. ZERO shots (the control fired two and hit twice), no
#   detonation, and the intruder reaches its waypoints at t=379.5.
#   Read together with sortie 01 this is the campaign's cleanest trade and it is not a close one: the
#   emission is what makes this aircraft exist, and holding it until 19 km on a 435 m/s closure means
#   arriving at the merge with a target and no solution.

unit p5kna
  team hostile
  flight brk1 1
  module mig29
  spawn 47.30000 48.20000 8000 270.0 450
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 70
  set n019_mode rad
  set n019_emission off              # POWER-UP POSITION BY DOCTRINE: this jet starts silent
  set rwr on
  set iff_interrogator on
  set kols_mode ir
  set brief_sort left
  set store 3 r27r
  set store 4 r27r
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set task intercept
  set brief_gci 65 250 19 8
  set brief_gci 80 250 12 8
  set brief_gci 90 250 8 8
  wp 47.30000 47.60000 8000 450      # the briefed vector: what Search flies while blind
  objective kill team friendly
  objective protect unit p5cp
  objective survive

unit p5knb
  team hostile
  flight brk1 2
  module mig29
  spawn 47.31200 48.21500 8150 270.0 450
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 70
  set n019_mode rad
  set n019_emission off
  set rwr on
  set iff_interrogator on
  set kols_mode ir
  set brief_sort left
  set store 3 r27r
  set store 4 r27r
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set task intercept
  set brief_gci 65 250 19 8
  set brief_gci 80 250 12 8
  set brief_gci 90 250 8 8
  wp 47.30000 47.60000 8000 450
  objective kill team friendly
  objective protect unit p5cp
  objective survive

unit p5int
  team friendly
  module f16
  spawn 47.15500 47.61500 8000 50.0 450
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 70
  set fcr_mode off                   # it radiates NOTHING: every warning it gets came from the barrier
  set rwr on                         # the price meter
  set datalink off
  wp 47.67500 48.53000 8000 450      # running in on the defended complex

unit p5ews
  module p18
  team hostile
  spawn 47.55000 48.20000 ground 0.0 0
  set emcon free

unit p5cp
  module target_hard
  team hostile
  spawn 47.67500 48.53000 ground 0.0 0

unit p5veh
  module target_soft
  team hostile
  spawn 47.68500 48.52000 ground 0.0 0
